WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:00.890
Wait…

2
00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:03.490
how did my phone know I was

3
00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:04.550
going to type “ready”?

4
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:09.160
Today: what AI really is — and how to

5
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.120
spot it in things you already use.

6
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.720
Some machines only follow fixed

7
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:16.200
steps that a person wrote.

8
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.600
This solar lamp has a timer: at

9
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.220
six o’clock, switch on.

10
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.000
Every day, exactly the same.

11
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.840
It never learns anything new.

12
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.020
Your phone keyboard is different.

13
00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.310
Nobody wrote a rule that says “after

14
00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:35.870
timetable is, suggest ready.”

15
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.300
Instead, it learned from a huge number

16
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.100
of examples of how people write.

17
00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.650
It spotted a pattern — and

18
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:44.950
made a best guess.

19
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.780
So here is our one-sentence version.

20
00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.050
Most AI today is a computer system

21
00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.010
that learns patterns from examples,

22
00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.110
then uses them to make predictions —

23
00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.770
best guesses — or decisions.

24
00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:01.170
Think of a new class.

25
00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.480
On day one you use the

26
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:04.920
seating chart — a rule.

27
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.340
A few weeks later you know every

28
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:09.840
face, even at the market.

29
00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:11.650
You learned from examples.

30
00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:13.590
Quick check!

31
00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.070
This solar lamp switches on

32
00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:17.930
at six every evening.

33
00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:19.690
Is that AI?

34
00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:21.870
Not AI.

35
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:25.800
It follows one fixed rule a person set.

36
00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.600
Useful — but it doesn’t learn.

37
00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.110
Once you know the pattern,

38
00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.170
you’ll spot AI everywhere.

39
00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.490
Voice typing that turns

40
00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:36.810
your speech into text.

41
00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:40.050
Spam filters that move “You won a prize!”

42
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.380
messages out of your inbox.

43
00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.060
Apps that look at a photo of a

44
00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.540
cassava leaf and warn about disease.

45
00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.220
Flood forecasts that warn

46
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:51.820
communities days ahead.

47
00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.980
Each one learned from lots of examples.

48
00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.100
And some things that are usually not

49
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.460
called AI: a calculator, a traffic light

50
00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.200
on a fixed timer, an automatic school

51
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:04.500
bell.

52
00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:06.870
They follow fixed steps.

53
00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:08.360
They don’t learn.

54
00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:10.210
Your turn.

55
00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.500
Which one of these uses AI?

56
00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.490
Voice typing — yes!

57
00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.600
Remember: rules are written by people.

58
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:20.980
AI learns from examples.

59
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.940
And what AI gives you is a

60
00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.900
prediction — a best guess.

61
00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.810
Try it in class tomorrow: draw two

62
00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:31.930
boxes on the board — learns from

63
00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.330
examples, and follows a rule.

64
00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.260
Call out everyday things and let your

65
00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.700
pupils decide, and explain why.
